AU  - Chung, Sun Wook
AU  - Kwon, Hyunji
VL  - 60
IS  - 1
SP  - 101595
AB  - How does a multinational enterprise (MNE) monitor and regulate the working conditions of a multi-tiered global value chain? Literature based on internalization theory focuses on the role of an MNE as the sole regulator, assuming the passivity of suppliers within the chain. We challenge this approach as it is not feasible for a single firm to monitor and regulate hundreds of independent suppliers. We expand on this literature, particularly the cascading governance thesis, by considering suppliers as regulatory intermediaries and addressing the issue of regulatory quality. Through a case study of two Korean multinational first-tier suppliers in the apparel and electronics industries, we find that an MNE, as a lead firm, engages in a more prudent form of cascading governance than originally theorized, maintaining direct audits of sub-suppliers. (2017, 2020). Second, the current theorization of cascading governance does not reflect the effects of cascading on regulatory quality. Addressing this gap is critical because the legitimacy of the cascading governance system can be jeopardized by irresponsible labor practices at any node of the global value chain (Alexander, 2022; Villena & Gioia, 2018). Therefore, we undertook a case study of two Korean MNEs that function as first-tier suppliers (one in the apparel industry and the other in the consumer electronics industry) working with lead firms that are global brands in their respective sectors. We interviewed forty-five managers responsible for compliance with the lead firm's labor requirements and related functions, such as production, sales, and sub-supplier management, from 2018 to 2021. We also analyzed internal company documents and various secondary data sources concerning the case companies, their clients, and private labor regulations in their respective industries. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. First, we review internalization theory and recent attempts to extend its application to understand how MNEs govern labor
