TY  - JOUR
TI  - The limits of firm-level globalization: Revisiting the FSA/CSA matrix
JO  - International Business Review
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1016/j.ibusrev.2016.01.018
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ibusrev.2016.01.018
AU  - Hillemann, Jenny
AU  - Gestrin, Michael
VL  - 25
IS  - 3
SP  - 767-775
AB  - Multinational enterprises (MNEs) engaging in foreign direct investment (FDI) need advantages allowing them to offset the liability of foreignness in host countries. This liability of foreignness gives rise to additional operational costs related to economic, institutional, and cultural differences between home and host countries. MNEs therefore need to own or control firm-specific advantages (FSAs) that, along with country-specific advantages (CSAs) and internalization advantages, affect international business transactions. In this paper, we revise Rugman’s classic FSA/CSA matrix to better reflect how firms bundle their assets with CSAs. We further contribute to the prior debate on the linkages between the global factory paradigm and internalization theory by empirically evaluating the validity of a key proposition associated with the global factory, namely that FDI becomes relatively less important as a building block of the modern MNE. To assure the optimal coordination across the MNE network, effective managerial planning is needed across the headquarters and foreign subsidiaries. Buckley and colleagues (Buckley, 2009a, Buckley, 2009b, Buckley, 2010, Buckley, 2011, Buckley, 2012, Buckley and Strange, 2015) describe such a system with internationally dispersed activities as a global factory. In this paper, we incorporate all three units of analysis and examine interactions between country-, firm- and subsidiary-level determinants explaining the choice to engage in FDI and ultimately the international architecture of the modern MNE. In doing so, we build upon Alan Rugman’s intellectual legacy and contribute to the IB literature in two ways: * • First, we revisit Rugman’s (1981, 2006) classic FSA/CSA matrix, adapting it to better reflect how firms bundle their assets with host country CSAs. By emphasizing the efficiency of markets for FSAs and CSAs (as opposed to the autonomous qualities of these) this adapted FSA/CSA matrix can provide a more intuitive explanation of the mix of modalities used by MNEs to organize themselves
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effects of local legitimacy on certification decisions to global and national CSR standards by multinational subsidiaries and domestic firms
JO  - Journal of International Business Studies
PY  - 2016
DO  - 10.1057/jibs.2016.3
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1057/jibs.2016.3
AU  - Husted, Bryan W
AU  - Montiel, Ivan
AU  - Christmann, Petra
VL  - 47
IS  - 3
SP  - 382-397
AB  - We explore the impact of local legitimacy on the imitation of certification by subsidiaries of foreign multinational enterprises and domestic firms. We propose that MNE (multinational enterprise) subsidiaries and domestic firms differ in their propensity to imitate geographically proximate firms when deciding whether to adopt national vs global CSR (corporate social responsibility) certifications for two reasons. First, there are differences in the legitimacy they can expect to gain in different communities from adopting these certifications. Second, there are differences in their knowledge about the local legitimacy of these certifications. We test our hypotheses by studying the decisions of automotive suppliers in Mexico to certify either to ISO 14001, a global certification, or to Clean Industry, a national certification. We find that geography matters: MNE subsidiaries imitate national certifications by geographically proximate firms to overcome a liability of foreignness, while domestic firms imitate global certifications by proximate firms to overcome the disadvantages of localness. We explore the implications of our findings for institutional theory and future research.
ER  -

